# Scaling Task Vectors in the Large-Delta Regime — Results

Empirical study of catastrophic merge failure and its recovery via task-vector
scaling, comparing a **large-delta** (continued-pretraining) model pair against a
**small-delta** (clean-SFT) control pair.

Merge method: plain Task Arithmetic — `theta_merged = theta_base + k_A * delta_A + k_B * delta_B`.
Each sweep scales one delta (`k` swept) while holding the other at 1.0.

---

## Task-vector magnitudes (per-parameter mean |delta| vs. base)

| Pair | Model | mean \|delta\| | Regime |
|------|-------|--------------|--------|
| Qwen | Qwen2.5-Math-7B-Instruct  | 0.016086 | large (continued pretraining) |
| Qwen | Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct | 0.014813 | large (continued pretraining) |
| Mistral | Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.1    | 0.000469 | small (clean SFT) |
| Mistral | OpenHermes-2.5-Mistral-7B   | 0.000061 | small (clean SFT) |

The large-delta task vectors are **~30–260x larger** than the small-delta ones —
a 1–2 order-of-magnitude gap between continued-pretraining and clean-SFT adaptation.

Base models: Qwen2.5-7B (Qwen pair); Mistral-7B-v0.1 (Mistral pair). Both bases
verified by key alignment (Qwen 339/339 keys, 0 mismatches; Mistral 291/291 keys).
The Qwen magnitude of 0.016086 reproduced an earlier independent measurement exactly.

